  "grading_reason": "The candidate correctly reproduces the entire reference diagnosis, including the lymphoma subtype, non–germinal-center immunophenotype, CNS and cutaneous involvement, and secondary hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH). Skin biopsies demonstrate large CD20/PAX5/CD79a-positive B cells confined to vascular lumina; MUM1 positivity, CD10 negativity, and weak BCL6 expression support the stated immunophenotype. Multifocal cerebral infarcts and encephalopathy support CNS involvement. Fever, splenomegaly, elevated ferritin and soluble IL-2 receptor, hypertriglyceridemia, and marrow hemophagocytosis support associated HLH.\n\nHowever, the added diagnosis of **secondary hypogammaglobulinemia is unsupported** by either the narrative or the ordered tests. No quantitative IgG, IgA, or IgM measurements or documented reduction in the gamma-globulin fraction are provided. Negative serum protein electrophoresis/immunofixation for a monoclonal protein and normal serum free light chains do **not** establish hypogammaglobulinemia. Although this complication can occur with B-cell malignancies or their treatment, its occurrence cannot be assumed here.\n\nThus, the core diagnosis is fully correct, but the candidate adds an unsubstantiated clinical diagnosis. This fits the rubric’s **correct diagnosis plus incorrect additions** category rather than a perfect match with a documented complication.",
  "grading_output": "**REASONING**: The candidate correctly reproduces the entire reference diagnosis, including the lymphoma subtype, non–germinal-center immunophenotype, CNS and cutaneous involvement, and secondary hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH). Skin biopsies demonstrate large CD20/PAX5/CD79a-positive B cells confined to vascular lumina; MUM1 positivity, CD10 negativity, and weak BCL6 expression support the stated immunophenotype. Multifocal cerebral infarcts and encephalopathy support CNS involvement. Fever, splenomegaly, elevated ferritin and soluble IL-2 receptor, hypertriglyceridemia, and marrow hemophagocytosis support associated HLH.\n\nHowever, the added diagnosis of **secondary hypogammaglobulinemia is unsupported** by either the narrative or the ordered tests. No quantitative IgG, IgA, or IgM measurements or documented reduction in the gamma-globulin fraction are provided. Negative serum protein electrophoresis/immunofixation for a monoclonal protein and normal serum free light chains do **not** establish hypogammaglobulinemia. Although this complication can occur with B-cell malignancies or their treatment, its occurrence cannot be assumed here.\n\nThus, the core diagnosis is fully correct, but the candidate adds an unsubstantiated clinical diagnosis. This fits the rubric’s **correct diagnosis plus incorrect additions** category rather than a perfect match with a documented complication.\n\n**SCORE**: 3",
